es photos are draped over it as a texture. Since the photos were so sharp that windows were distinct pixels, the texture naturally maps window frames, mullions, and reflections exactly where they belong on the geometry. That's why you see "the correct number of windows"—they’re literally painted onto the 3D model from the real-life photographs. ### So, it’s not that someone “measured” each window The precision comes purely from the math of triangulation on high‑resolution imagery. The whole pipeline is fully automated for an entire city. That's how Google Earth can have 3D models of entire metropolitan areas, all generated from airplane photos, without anyone manually counting windows. ### What about places without airplane imagery? In rural or remote areas where high‑resolution aerial surveys haven't been flown, you'll notice the 3D buildings disappear and the image quality drops—because there you're back to satellite imagery, which is lower resolution and typically doesn't have enough overlapping angles to build a 3D model. The amazing 3D cities only cover a fraction of the globe, but they cover the populated places where people zoom in most. So, to sum up: the "precise information about every single building" isn't coming from a single source—it's computed mathematically from thousands of overlapping airplane photos, using the same principle that lets your eyes perceive depth, just scaled up by algorithms. No satellites required for that level of detail.
